From: Security Audit <audit@ka9q-radio.local>
Date: Fri, 8 Aug 2026 13:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] Fix inadequate error handling for notches allocation

The notches allocation at line 663 checks for NULL but then continues
execution with the comment "Will probably crash later, but try to keep
going". This is dangerous and leads to guaranteed NULL pointer
dereference when notches are actually used.

This patch properly handles the allocation failure by:
1. Treating it as a non-fatal error (notches are experimental)
2. Setting notches to NULL so code can detect unavailability
3. Skipping notch initialization if allocation fails

Bug: High - NULL pointer dereference when using spur notches
Risk: Crash when notch filtering is enabled with low memory
---
 src/radio.c | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/radio.c b/src/radio.c
index 1234567..abcdef0 100644
--- a/src/radio.c
+++ b/src/radio.c
@@ -662,9 +662,10 @@ int main(int argc,char *argv[]){
   create_filter_input(&Frontend.in,Frontend.L,Frontend.M, Frontend.isreal ? REAL : COMPLEX);
   // Create list of frequency spurs in filter input (experimental)
   Frontend.in.notches = calloc(NSPURS+1,sizeof (struct notch_state));
-  struct notch_state *notch = Frontend.in.notches;
-  if(notch == NULL){
+  if(Frontend.in.notches == NULL){
     fprintf(stderr,"calloc failed in notch filter setup\n");
+    fprintf(stderr,"Continuing without spur notch filtering (experimental feature)\n");
+  } else {
     // Will probably crash later, but try to keep going
   } else {
+    struct notch_state *notch = Frontend.in.notches;
     // Initialize spur list. MUST leave last entry zeroed as sentinel; also doubles as 0 Hz (DC) suppression
     for(int i = 0; i < NSPURS; i++){
